{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/189974-fenris-ur-and-norse-mythology","id":189974,"title":"Fenris-Ur and Norse mythology","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-07-07T00:04:34-07:00","id":189974,"title":"Fenris-Ur and Norse mythology","content":"As most of you know, I run game placed in northern Sweden, on beginning of Lapland. I use localized version of Wolf and Moon couple creation myth, in that Mother of Wolves - <b>Angerboda</b> - had werewolf children with <b>Mani</b>, Father Moon ( in spirit of coming <b>Idigam Chronicle</b> and it's take on gender of Divine Parents ). The problem is, of course, with role of <b>Fenris-Ur</b> in that take on story.<br /> <br /> In Norse myth <b>Angerboda</b> grows to be jaded/evil giantess that with Loki ( or other beings ) have monster children, like <b>Fenris</b> or <span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">world serpent </span></span></span>J\u00f6rmungandr. But in myths, Wolf Destroyer and his brood want to devoure Moon and then the world:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wikipedia</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">(...)<span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Fenrir is the father of the wolves </span></span></span><a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sk%C3%B6ll\" target=\"_blank\">Sk\u00f6ll</a><span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"> and </span></span></span><a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hati_Hr%C3%B3%C3%B0vitnisson\" target=\"_blank\">Hati Hr\u00f3\u00f0vitnisson</a><span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">, is a son of </span></span></span><a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Loki\" target=\"_blank\">Loki</a><span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">, and is foretold to kill the god </span></span></span><a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Odin\" target=\"_blank\">Odin</a><span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"> during the events of </span></span></span><a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ragnar%C3%B6k\" target=\"_blank\">Ragnar\u00f6k</a><span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">, but will in turn be killed by Odin's son </span></span></span><a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/V%C3%AD%C3%B0arr\" target=\"_blank\">V\u00ed\u00f0arr</a><span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">.</span></span></span> <span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">In the <i>Prose Edda</i>, additional information is given about Fenrir, including that, due to the gods' knowledge of prophecies foretelling great trouble from Fenrir and his rapid growth, the gods bound him, and as a result Fenrir bit off the right hand of the god<a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/T%C3%BDr\" target=\"_blank\">T\u00fdr</a>. Depictions of Fenrir have been identified on various objects, and scholarly theories have been proposed regarding Fenrir's relation to other canine beings in Norse mythology. Fenrir has been the subject of artistic depictions, and he appears in literature.</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\"><br /> <br /> And<br /> <br /> </span></span></span><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wikipedia</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">In </span></span></span><a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norse_mythology\" target=\"_blank\">Norse mythology</a><span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">, </span></span></span><b>Hati Hr\u00f3\u00f0vitnisson</b><span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"> (first name meaning &quot;He Who Hates, Enemy&quot;</span></span></span><sup><a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hati_Hr%C3%B3%C3%B0vitnisson#cite_note-BYOPRO164-1\" target=\"_blank\">[1]</a></sup><span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">) is a wolf that according to </span></span></span><i><a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gylfaginning\" target=\"_blank\">Gylfaginning</a></i><span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"> chases the Moon across the night sky, just as the wolf </span></span></span><a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sk%C3%B6ll\" target=\"_blank\">Sk\u00f6ll</a><span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"> chases the Sun during the day, until the time of</span></span></span><a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ragnar%C3%B6k\" target=\"_blank\">Ragnar\u00f6k</a><span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"> when they will swallow these heavenly bodies, after which </span></span></span><a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fenrir\" target=\"_blank\">Fenrir</a><span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"> will break free from his bonds and kill </span></span></span><a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Odin\" target=\"_blank\">Odin</a><span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">. Hat\u00ed is possibly alluded to in </span></span></span><i><a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/V%C3%B6lusp%C3%A1\" target=\"_blank\">V\u00f6lusp\u00e1</a></i><span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"> as &quot;</span></span></span><i>moon-snatcher</i><span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">&quot;.</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\"><br /> <br /> And those theories as interpretations<br /> <br /> </span></span></span><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In reference to Fenrir's presentation in the <i>Prose Edda</i>, Andy Orchard theorizes that &quot;the hound (or wolf)&quot; <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garmr\" target=\"_blank\">Garmr</a>, <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sk%C3%B6ll\" target=\"_blank\">Sk\u00f6ll</a>, and <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hati_Hr%C3%B3%C3%B0vitnisson\" target=\"_blank\">Hati Hr\u00f3\u00f0vitnisson</a> were originally simply all Fenrir, stating that &quot;Snorri, characteristically, is careful to make distinctions, naming the wolves who devour the sun and moon as Sk\u00f6ll and Hati Hr\u00f3\u00f0vitnisson respectively, and describing an encounter between Garm and T\u00fdr (who, one would have thought, might like to get his hand on Fenrir) at Ragnar\u00f6k.&quot;<sup><a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fenrir#cite_note-ORCHARD43-40\" target=\"_blank\">[40]</a></sup> </div> </div> </div></span></span></span><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">John Lindow says that it is unclear why the gods decide to raise Fenrir as opposed to his siblings Hel and J\u00f6rmungandr in <i>Gylfaginning</i> chapter 35, theorizing that it may be &quot;because Odin had a connection with wolves? Because Loki was Odin's blood brother?&quot; Referring to the same chapter, Lindow comments that neither of the phrases that Fenrir's binding result in have left any other traces. Lindow compares Fenrir's role to his father Loki and Fenrir's brother J\u00f6rmungandr, in that they all spend time with the gods, are bound or cast out by them, return &quot;at the end of the current mythic order to destroy them, only to be destroyed himself as a younger generation of gods, one of them his slayer, survives into the new world order.</span></span></span> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:10px\">So how to </span>reconcile<span style=\"font-size:10px\"> logical myth gods analogies ( Angerboda and Mani ), Fenris position in it and Norse myths? We can assume that mythology was created by humans listening to Shadows gods posing as </span><a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%86sir\" target=\"_blank\">\u00c6sir</a>, with <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/135690-spirits-and-gods?p=177316#post177316\"><b>All-Father-Thunder</b></a> ( Great Elemental Incarna of Storms and Protection ) working as Odin. But still, from logical standpoint Fenris Ur should be in total opposition to Father Moon, not working with him on Uratha patronage. So how to work them in alliance to form Blood Talons Tribe?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-07T00:14:35-07:00","id":189991,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Werewolf: the Forsaken - Lodges: the Faithful, page 15-16, &quot;Human Cultures&quot; sidebar</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Many lodges seem to have some basis in a specific human culture, be they based on a culture\u2019s myths, legends or merely the customs of the people. The Lodge of Wendigo is an example of this, bonded as lodge members are bonded to a spirit that Native Americans view as a cannibal creature from the spirit world. When designing your own lodge, what kind of balance needs to be struck between human belief, mythology or history and the Uratha mindset? If a particular legend or cultural custom strikes you as interesting, then run with it. <b>Just remember that chances are the Uratha are likely to see many more levels of depth to any \u201cmonster legend\u201d that humans have told for centuries.</b> In Norse mythology, Garm is a wolf creature who is destined to kill Tyr, the God of War, at Ragnarok, the battle of the end of the world. To the werewolves who follow Garm as a totem, he has a rich history that humanity can never know about, while his foretold \u201cdestiny\u201d in human myth is a complete misinterpretation of his past. <b>Any lodge that has elements of human culture inherent within its own outlook or customs will likely have similar variants on the stories that humans are familiar with. Humanity is blind to much of the world\u2019s shadow-truths, and lodges displaying elements of human culture or mythology highlight this fact all the more.</b><br /> <br /> But what if the reverse is also true? What if some lodges are, in fact, the very reason some myths exist in human culture? A compelling idea is to create a lodge that \u2014 by its purpose and history \u2014 has actually spawned mythology surrounding it. What of the bunyip myths in Australia or the Wild Hunt of Celtic legend? What if these stories came into being purely because of the werewolves who created them? <b>It\u2019s a neat idea that some lodges could just as easily be the source of human myths, rather than simply being reflections of them.</b></div> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"color:#FFFFFF\">characterlimit</span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-07T01:23:51-07:00","id":190020,"title":"","content":"I think you may be wanting to read this book here: <a href=\"http://rpg.drivethrustuff.com/product/2514/Tribebook-Get-of-Fenris-Revised?term=get+of+fenris&amp;it=1\" target=\"_blank\">http://rpg.drivethrustuff.com/produc...of+fenris&amp;it=1</a><br /> <br /> I think though there is already reconciliation with the Fenrir tales in the forsaken core books. Basically humanity boned it up.<br /> <br /> Ok re-reading your post, here's a write up. Loki is a shape shifter. He changes shape often. Father wolf being the great equalizer of the world made enemies, and would be viciously slandered by the spirits and flesh creatures of the world that he maintained. So Loki fathered Fenrir to be a world destroyer in humanities eyes, as influenced by their 'gods' (spirits that claimed people, and or manifested to corrupt their world view against the Uratha.). Try that take on it.<br /> <br /> I'm really tired at the moment so my posts may not make the most sense, but I can't NOT post in a thread on the wolf god. Yeah yeah he isnt exactly diety status, but he's as much a god as Loki is (and he damn well isn't). Same for Skadi. All Jotunn, so where the actual godhood comes from I could not say.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /> WoD-Dark Eras!! (Backed for Viking Age Werewolf)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1939-lyrics-of-war","name":"Lyrics Of War","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1939_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1769"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-07T02:04:51-07:00","id":190027,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lyrics Of War</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/189974-fenris-ur-and-norse-mythology?p=190020#post190020\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ok re-reading your post, here's a write up. Loki is a shape shifter. He changes shape often. Father wolf being the great equalizer of the world made enemies, and would be viciously slandered by the spirits and flesh creatures of the world that he maintained. So Loki fathered Fenrir to be a world destroyer in humanities eyes, as influenced by their 'gods' (spirits that claimed people, and or manifested to corrupt their world view against the Uratha.). Try that take on it.<br /> <br /> I'm really tired at the moment so my posts may not make the most sense, but I can't NOT post in a thread on the wolf god. Yeah yeah he isnt exactly diety status, but he's as much a god as Loki is (and he damn well isn't). Same for Skadi. All Jotunn, so where the actual godhood comes from I could not say.</div> </div> </div> </div>So you proposing the Loki being Father Wolf. But in Norse mythology there is male Moon God called Mani. This is why I proposed Angerboda as Wolf-Mother, because:<br /> <br /> 1. She is mother of many monsters, few of them are wolves.<br /> <br /> 2. In some myths she could become She-Wolf at will.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-07T05:02:16-07:00","id":190145,"title":"","content":"It could be as simple as humans hearing the word somehow, and associocated with dangerous wolves. When it came to myths, they used the word fenris for a dangerous wolf. Because Fenris-Ur is sometimes translated as Destroyer wolf, just like Hikaon-Ur is Black Wolf. So the word, at least in Forsaken world, excisted before humans used it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-07T11:48:48-07:00","id":190571,"title":"","content":"Could well be there was some d-bag human named Wodan who pissed off a Blood Talon arrogant enough to call himself Fenrir with the milestone gift. Swallows Odin, gets killed later and bam humans have a story that they won't soon forget. Is lunacy still a thing in Forsaken?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /> WoD-Dark Eras!! (Backed for Viking Age Werewolf)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1939-lyrics-of-war","name":"Lyrics Of War","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1939_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1769"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-07T19:13:37-07:00","id":191018,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wyrdhamster</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/189974-fenris-ur-and-norse-mythology?p=190027#post190027\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> So you proposing the Loki being Father Wolf. But in Norse mythology there is male Moon God called Mani. This is why I proposed Angerboda as Wolf-Mother, because:<br /> <br /> 1. She is mother of many monsters, few of them are wolves.<br /> <br /> 2. In some myths she could become She-Wolf at will.</div> </div> </div> </div>They're gods and spirits. You can change the rules of reproduction too.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I call the Integrity-analogue the &quot;<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/822559-trying-to-understand-the-morality-system?p=825008#post825008\" target=\"_blank\">subjective stat</a>&quot;.<br /> An explanation how to <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/913221-using-social-manuevering\" target=\"_blank\">use Social Manuevering</a>.<br /> Guanxi Explanations: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=787824#post787824\" target=\"_blank\">1</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=788173#post788173\" target=\"_blank\">2</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/833863-mages-and-trading-favors?p=834531#post834531\" target=\"_blank\">3</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2758-errol216","name":"Errol216","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1112"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-08T00:26:07-07:00","id":191181,"title":"","content":"I think more plausible is that there is Wolf Mother instead of Father Wolf in myths than Moon Father gave birth to children... ( I don't have a problem with trans people, just humanity - and I think werewolves also - are more inclined to sustain mainstream take on reproduction ) Especially, as devs are supporting the notion of interchanging gender of Divine Parents of Uratha in <b>Idigam Chronicle</b>.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1437,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","user":"wyrdhamster","date":". 07-08-2014, 03:28 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-08T02:51:50-07:00","id":191271,"title":"","content":"Well, remeber that Loki did give birth to Sleipnir, for example, after asuming the shape of a mare. Why couldn't something similar had happened more than once?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><b>I</b>'m <b>S</b>o <b>M</b>eta <b>E</b>ven <b>T</b>his <b>A</b>cronym</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/195-thorbes","name":"Thorbes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar195_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2982"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-08T04:00:00-07:00","id":191289,"title":"","content":"Why would Angrbo\u00f0a be worse than Loki as &quot;Wolf Parent&quot; of the race? Loki was later married to Sig, wasn't him? He weren't jaded and angry, or just weak, lie Father Wolf was in original Uratha myth. Angerboda was known for her spite, being called &quot;giantess&quot; from other race, and to assume shapes of wolf. She is also mostly mysterious to modern researches. What have Loki more than her as Wolf Parent?<br /> <br /> Angrbo\u00f0a'sWikiepdia states:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wikipedia</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> <span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Snorri's <i><a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gylfaginning\" target=\"_blank\">Gylfaginning</a></i> gives a prose explanation and a variant form of these stanzas. Brodeur's translation renders:</span></span></span><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> A witch dwells to the east of <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midgard\" target=\"_blank\">Midgard</a>, in the forest called Ironwood: in that wood dwell the troll-women, who are known as Ironwood-Women [<i>I\u00e1rnvidjur</i>]. The old witch bears many giants for sons, and all in the shape of wolves; and from this source are these wolves sprung. The saying runs thus: from this race shall come one that shall be mightiest of all, he that is named Moon-Hound [<i>M\u00e1nagarm</i>]; he shall be filled with the flesh of all those men that die, and he shall swallow the moon, and sprinkle with blood the heavens and all the lair; thereof-shall the sun lose her shining, and the winds in that day shall be unquiet and roar on every side. So it says in <i>V\u00f6lusp\u00e1</i>:<br /> </div></div> </div> </div> </div>Even in Old Norse <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_Norse_language\" target=\"_blank\">h</a>er name means<span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"> &quot;the one who brings grief&quot;</span></span></span><span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"> or &quot;she-who-offers-sorrow&quot;</span></span></span><span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11px\">. </span></span></span>Isn't this strike more as original Father Wolf from <b>Forsaken</b> corebook near his end of life? \u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-08T06:22:21-07:00","id":191331,"title":"","content":"Nah, it was just a comment that it could also work, gender is not that important.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><b>I</b>'m <b>S</b>o <b>M</b>eta <b>E</b>ven <b>T</b>his <b>A</b>cronym</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/195-thorbes","name":"Thorbes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar195_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2982"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-09T02:53:20-07:00","id":192215,"title":"","content":"Wait wait, WHERE in the lore does it say that she can shapeshift into a wolf? And she gave birth to Fenrir, Jormungandr AND Hell. There is nothing in the lore indicating that Angrboda gave birth to numberous wolves. More to the point, Fenrir banged some random that isn't directly mentioned. That is where Hati and Skoll come from. It does NOT say that it is from Angrboda. I would also be careful with information from wikipedia and not scholarly sources. There is a shit ton of speculation out there on nordic culture (the asatru community is certainly NOT helping the issue... the Wiccatru are even worse), not a lot of it is helpful. <br /> <br /> As for father wolf. He wasn't jaded, or weak persay. At the start he was strong, and I would argue he moved with the motives of the spirit wilds, not human emotions. He is a spirit after all. The first pack noticed it was taking him longer to kill his prey so they took it upon themselves to end him and take the mantel of guardian upon themselves. It's more likely the Forsaken jumped the gun then he was as weak as they say.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /> WoD-Dark Eras!! (Backed for Viking Age Werewolf)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1939-lyrics-of-war","name":"Lyrics Of War","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1939_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1769"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-12T02:43:47-07:00","id":195348,"title":"","content":"I support the &quot;humanity screwed up the story&quot; routine. Fenris-Ur is terrifying, he didn't earn the name Destroyer Wolf for nothing. The Norse people catch sight of the Destroyer, proclaim him a rampant force of destruction and therefore evil. Humanity then encounters some vicious Rahu and notice they rage and the moon is full and they get whacked by the lunacy stick. Fenris-Ur, the Destroyer rages at the moon and wants to eat it. The Uratha keep this myth going, because it lets Fenris-Ur and the blood talons have their space.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2861-nyx","name":"Nyx","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2861_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"261"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}